# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Every runtime setting of the aicc server, with its built-in default.
#
#   cp .env.example .env   # then uncomment only what this deployment changes
#
# The server reads .env from its working directory at startup and never
# overrides a real environment variable with it (internal/config/config.go).
# Lines beginning with # are ignored; surrounding single or double quotes are
# stripped from values. There is no inline-comment syntax — everything after
# the first = is the value, so keep notes on their own line.
#
# Every line below is commented out and shows the value the server already
# uses, so a copied file changes nothing until something is uncommented — and
# a default that changes in a later release still reaches this deployment.
#
# **An empty value means "unset"**: the server falls back to the default, it
# does not read it as "blank". A setting whose default is non-empty therefore
# cannot be cleared by assigning nothing to it (AICC_LOG_DIR= keeps writing to
# logs/); give it a different value instead.

#
# Process
#
# AICC_ENV must be dev or prod. dev logs as text and is more verbose; prod logs
# JSON. It does not by itself turn on secure cookies — see AICC_SECURE_COOKIES.
#AICC_ENV=dev
# The API, the event stream and the embedded SPA.
#AICC_HTTP_ADDR=:8080
# Separate unauthenticated listener: /metrics, /healthz, /readyz. Bound to
# loopback by default — it has no authentication of its own.
#AICC_METRICS_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9090

#
# Database — PostgreSQL 18. Migrations run at startup.
#
#AICC_DATABASE_URL=postgres://aicc:aicc@127.0.0.1:5432/aicc?sslmode=disable
# Pool size. Must be >= 1 or the server refuses to start.
#AICC_DATABASE_MAX_CONNS=10

#
# FreeSWITCH
#
# ESL is on 18021 here, not the stock 8021.
#AICC_ESL_ADDR=127.0.0.1:18021
#AICC_ESL_PASSWORD=ClueCon
# Must match FreeSWITCH's own $${domain}: it is the realm a phone registers to
# and the domain an endpoint is dialled at. A queue name does not carry it. If
# the box moves (DHCP), this and vars.xml have to move together.
#AICC_SWITCH_DOMAIN=127.0.0.1
# The sofia profile agents register to.
#AICC_SIP_PROFILE=internal
# The digest realm, and the domain a browser phone builds its address of record
# from. The internal profile challenges with challenge-realm=auto_from, so the
# realm the phone is asked for is the domain it put in its From header — this
# one — and md5(extension:AICC_SIP_DOMAIN:password) is the hash that verifies.
# Empty means AICC_SWITCH_DOMAIN, which is right until a deployment separates
# the two.
#AICC_SIP_DOMAIN=127.0.0.1
# The WebSocket binding a browser phone connects to: the internal profile's
# ws-binding. Must be ws:// or wss://. Empty means ws://<AICC_SIP_DOMAIN>:5066/.
#AICC_SIP_WSS_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:5066/

#
# AI voice leg — the in-process SIP UAS the switch bridges bot calls to.
# Set AICC_BOT_ENABLED=false for a deployment that only routes to people.
#
#AICC_BOT_ENABLED=true
# Where the bot's SIP listens: the target of the switch's aicc_bot gateway.
# Each call's SDP answer and its RTP/RTCP sockets use the address the switch sent
# the INVITE to, so with 0.0.0.0 the media ports still bind only that address.
#AICC_BOT_SIP_HOST=0.0.0.0
#AICC_BOT_SIP_PORT=6060
# Addresses allowed to send SIP to the bot and RTP to its calls: comma-separated
# IPs and CIDR prefixes. Anything else is dropped without a response, OPTIONS
# included, and logged at WARN. Empty allows every peer. List every address the
# switch sends from, signalling and media alike: the host's LAN address when it
# runs on this host, its compose network when it runs in the stack. Leave the
# switch out and its aicc_bot gateway goes down. A value that does not parse
# refuses to start.
#AICC_BOT_ALLOWED_PEERS=
# Even RTP / odd RTCP pairs. Deliberately clear of FreeSWITCH's own
# 16384-16584 because dev co-locates both on one host.
#AICC_BOT_RTP_PORT_LOW=40000
#AICC_BOT_RTP_PORT_HIGH=40999
# Concurrent AI calls the UAS accepts. 500 port pairs back the default 220.
#AICC_BOT_MAX_CALLS=220
# Of AICC_BOT_MAX_CALLS, how many an answered outbound call may not take: they
# stay for inbound. An outbound call reserves a slot only while in-use plus
# reserved is below MAX_CALLS minus this; inbound may use all of MAX_CALLS.
# 0 reserves nothing. Must be below AICC_BOT_MAX_CALLS or startup is refused.
#
# It is also what campaigns keep free for inbound (design 10 section 6.1). Set,
# it is the one number for both: campaign admission stops that many slots short
# of MAX_CALLS and so does every outbound reservation. Unset, campaign admission
# keeps max(2, 10% of AICC_BOT_MAX_CALLS) free (never more than leaves one slot
# for outbound), and a call that was already admitted and answers may still take
# any free slot, since refusing it would lose a lead for nothing; with 220 slots
# the default keeps 22 free for inbound. Inbound itself is never refused for it.
#AICC_BOT_INBOUND_RESERVE=
# How long an answered AI outbound call waits, parked in silence, for a bot slot
# to free before it is hung up with hangupCause OVERFLOW (no announcement).
# Go duration, 0 to 10s; 0 hangs up at once.
#AICC_OVERFLOW_HOLD=1500ms
# Overflow control (design 10 section 6.5). Per campaign, the share of its last
# 200 answered calls that met no bot (hangupCause OVERFLOW). Above the tighten
# rate the dialer plans with the 99% upper bound of the answer rate instead of
# the 95% one; above the stop rate it plans for every ringing leg to answer
# (no over-dial) until the rate is back under the tighten rate. Fractions:
# 0 < tighten < stop <= 1.
#AICC_OVERFLOW_TIGHTEN_RATE=0.01
#AICC_OVERFLOW_STOP_RATE=0.03
# A call whose inbound media was flowing and then stopped for this long is
# ended. Go duration; 0 disables. Does not apply before the first packet.
#AICC_BOT_RTP_DEAD_TIMEOUT=5s
# A call that has received no inbound media at all this long after the answer is
# ended. Long on purpose: a carrier may open its media path seconds after the
# answer. Go duration; 0 disables, leaving the caller's hangup to end the call.
#AICC_BOT_FIRST_MEDIA_TIMEOUT=30s
# Holds the bot's greeting until the caller's inbound media has started. The
# model session still opens at call start; only the request for the first turn
# waits. Unset (the default) greets the moment the session is ready, as every
# call did before this setting: on a carrier that opens its media path seconds
# after the answer, that greeting is spoken into a path that does not exist yet
# and the caller never hears it. Go duration. 0 waits for the first inbound RTP
# however long (AICC_BOT_FIRST_MEDIA_TIMEOUT still ends a call whose media never
# starts); a positive value waits for it or this long, whichever comes first;
# negative is refused. Per call, the logs say how long media took ("first
# media") and whether the first bot audio went out before it ("first bot
# audio", isBeforeMedia), whether or not this is set.
#AICC_BOT_GREETING_MEDIA_WAIT=
# Dead air: how long a call waits, from the moment the caller has heard the
# bot's last word (PLAYBACK_DONE), for speech, a keypress or a new bot turn
# before the flow gets NO_INPUT. With no authored NO_INPUT rule and a
# global.closingTarget, the third consecutive NO_INPUT ends the call, so a
# silent caller is given up on after about three times this plus the
# re-prompts. Go duration, 2s to 60s inclusive; there is no off. The log line
# "dead air" (afterMs=) shows the value in effect.
#AICC_BOT_NO_INPUT_TIMEOUT=8s
# Base URL that flows' declarative HTTP tools call. Empty disables them.
# Empty makes every one of those tools fail, which is the *correct* behaviour —
# a bot must not invent facts — and useless for walking a bot through a
# conversation, which needs a backend to be there. The repository ships one:
#   go run ./cmd/aicc-mockbackend -addr 127.0.0.1:8770
# It answers the interface the five reference flows in internal/seed/flows
# expect, from fixed data, so a walkthrough has a determinate right answer
# (RMA1001 is in repair, 92223333 is eight days overdue, and so on).
#
# The address comes only from here. A flow's own apiBaseEnv field does nothing.
# A tool may name a backend of its own with http.baseUrl; this one is used by
# every tool that does not.
#AICC_BOT_BACKEND_BASE=
# The public hosts a flow's HTTP tools may reach: the outbound host policy.
# Decided on the address a tool's host resolves to, as the connection is made:
# private and loopback addresses (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 127/8, ::1,
# fc00::/7) are reachable without an entry; a public address only when the
# tool's host is listed here; link-local (169.254/16, fe80::/10, the cloud
# metadata address among them), AWS's IPv6 metadata range fd00:ec2::/32 and
# unspecified addresses (0.0.0.0, ::) never, even when listed. The host of AICC_BOT_BACKEND_BASE is always allowed.
# Comma-separated entries: host, host:port, *.domain (subdomains only, not the
# domain itself) or an IP address ([v6]:port with a port). Empty lists nothing.
# Redirects are never followed. A refused connection fails the tool and logs
# "http tool connection refused by the outbound host policy" with host,
# address and rule.
#AICC_TOOL_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
# Secrets for HTTP tool headers. A flow never holds a credential: a header
# value references one as {secrets.NAME} (as in "Bearer {secrets.KB_API_KEY}"),
# and it resolves from AICC_TOOL_SECRET_<NAME>, NAME being capital letters,
# digits and underscores. The value is never stored in a flow, returned by the
# API or logged. Publishing a flow that references an unset one is refused;
# saving it as a draft only warns. Read when a call is made, from the process
# environment, so changing one takes a restart. No default; one per secret.
# Trust model: a secret is not tied to a host. Anyone who can write flows
# (config:write) can make a tool send any configured secret to any host the
# policy above lets tools reach (the LAN is open by default) and surface what
# that backend echoes, so config:write amounts to the use of every tool
# secret. Grant it only to people trusted with these secrets, and give each
# secret the least privilege at its backend.
# Example: the API key of the aicc-knowledge service (flow-authoring 4.2).
#AICC_TOOL_SECRET_KB_API_KEY=

#
# Voice providers
#
# The credential the selected provider profile reads — OPENAI_API_KEY for
# openai, XAI_API_KEY for grok, ALIYUN_API_KEY for qwen, REALTIME_API_KEY for
# gateway, DOUBAO_API_KEY for doubao. Required for the AI leg:
# a session fails to start without it. These are the providers' own names, not
# AICC_* settings. On gateway the credential is the one that gateway was
# configured to accept, and the engine's own key lives over there, not here.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
XAI_API_KEY=
ALIYUN_API_KEY=
REALTIME_API_KEY=
DOUBAO_API_KEY=
#
# Which provider answers every call in this deployment (phase1-decisions A1).
# It is chosen here, once, at startup — a call's language never selects it.
# qwen or doubao inside mainland China, openai or grok elsewhere; the vendors are not
# all reachable with acceptable latency from the same network. An unknown name
# refuses to start, and the refusal lists the accepted values (openai, grok,
# gateway, qwen, doubao). A kept .env naming a provider that has since been removed
# gets the same refusal: pick one of those.
#
# grok is xAI's Grok Voice (grok-voice-3.0), a dialect of the OpenAI Realtime
# protocol answered by the same client; the key is XAI_API_KEY. Like openai it
# takes telephone audio untouched. Its recogniser for the human phase is not
# xAI's: say AICC_TRANSCRIBE_PROVIDER (see below) when transcription is on.
#
# gateway is another value: a Realtime gateway of our own, composing ASR, an
# LLM and TTS behind this same protocol so an engine that never spoke Realtime
# can answer a call. From here it is a provider like any other, and it needs
# AICC_TRANSCRIBE_PROVIDER set explicitly (see below) — the human phase's
# recogniser is a separate client that the gateway does not serve.
#
# doubao is the fourth, and the only one that is not the Realtime protocol at
# all: ByteDance's full-duplex dialogue API, answered by a second client of its
# own. It needs AICC_TRANSCRIBE_PROVIDER said explicitly too, and for the same
# reason — with transcription on and that setting empty, the recogniser follows
# AICC_PROVIDER, and there is no doubao recognition client for it to find.
# Three things about it belong in a deployment's flows rather than here:
#   · the voice is a name from this vendor's own voice list, chosen per flow in
#     global.voice; the profile's default is zh_female_vv_jupiter_bigtts.
#   · every terminal phase must carry an "announce". Nothing this client sends
#     makes that engine take a turn, so a phase the call stops at with no words
#     of its own is a caller listening to silence — a publish is refused.
#   · an entry phase with no "announce" means the bot answers and waits for the
#     caller to speak first. That is a choice, not an oversight; say the
#     greeting in the entry phase's announce to open the call.
#
#AICC_PROVIDER=openai
#
# Where that provider is reached and which model answers there. Empty keeps the
# value built into the selected profile:
#   openai  wss://api.openai.com/v1/realtime            gpt-realtime-2.1
#   grok    wss://api.x.ai/v1/realtime                   grok-voice-3.0
#           The profile's voice is eve; a flow's global.voice wins.
#   qwen    wss://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/api-ws/v1/realtime
#           qwen-audio-3.1-realtime-plus (there is no flash variant). The
#           profile's voice is longanqian_v3.1.
#   gateway ws://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/realtime                cascade
#           Its own default listen address, plain ws:// (it serves no TLS).
#           The model name is echoed back and routes nothing: which engine
#           answers is the gateway's own configuration, not ours.
#   doubao  wss://openspeech.bytedance.com/api/v3/duplex/realtime/dialogue
#           1.2.6.1 — a protocol version, not a product name, pinned inside the
#           client. AICC_PROVIDER_MODEL is ignored here; which engine answers
#           is chosen in the vendor's console, against the application id the
#           key belongs to.
# Point the endpoint at a proxy, a regional host, or any server speaking the
# OpenAI Realtime protocol.
#AICC_PROVIDER_ENDPOINT=
#AICC_PROVIDER_MODEL=

# What the *conversation* session transcribes the caller with. Distinct from
# AICC_TRANSCRIBE_MODEL below, which belongs to the standalone recogniser used
# for the human phase; this one decides whether the caller's own words appear
# in the transcript of the bot phase at all.
#   openai  gpt-live-transcribe  — this vendor does not transcribe input unless
#           asked, so without it the bot phase's transcript is the bot's own
#           words and its tool traces, with nothing the caller said in it.
#   qwen    (none) — measured 2026-08-23: this dialect sends
#           conversation.item.input_audio_transcription.* unprompted.
#   gateway (none) — it accepts the field but its model and language only
#           echo; what recognises the caller is configured on the gateway.
#   doubao  does not apply — that protocol transcribes the caller unprompted
#           and its session payload has no field to ask in, so a value here is
#           read by nothing.
# The literal "off" disables it. An empty value means unset everywhere in this
# file, so it cannot blank a profile's own default — off has to be said.
#AICC_PROVIDER_TRANSCRIBE_MODEL=

#
# Live transcription of the human phase
#
# Off by default: it adds an external dependency, a listener and real spend, so
# doing nothing keeps today's behaviour. When it is on, the switch taps the
# agent's own leg at the bridge and streams it here.
#AICC_TRANSCRIPTION_ENABLED=false

# Where the tapped audio arrives. Loopback by default, following
# AICC_METRICS_ADDR: this carries unencrypted call audio, so a default binding
# every interface would be the mistake nobody notices. A containerised
# deployment binds the container address instead.
#AICC_STREAM_ADDR=127.0.0.1:8090

# What the *switch* dials back, which is not what we bind — in the demo stack
# that is the compose service name, exactly as AICC_ESL_ADDR already is. The
# scheme is part of this value and nothing composes one, so a deployment that
# needs wss:// changes configuration rather than code. Required when
# transcription is enabled; the demo stack's value is ws://aicc:8090/stream.
#AICC_STREAM_PUBLIC_URL=

# Signs the single-use token that is the whole of the authentication on that
# listener. Required when transcription is enabled.
#AICC_STREAM_SECRET=

# The recognition client. Empty follows AICC_PROVIDER, since one vendor is
# reachable per deployment — but they are separate settings, because the two
# engines speak different wire protocols and a deployment may legitimately
# converse with one and transcribe with the other.
#
# On AICC_PROVIDER=grok, gateway and doubao this must be said: all three
# are conversation endpoints and none is a recogniser, so following
# AICC_PROVIDER would look for a recognition client
# that does not exist and the server would refuse to start.
#AICC_TRANSCRIBE_PROVIDER=

# Diagnosis only: a directory the DashScope recognition client writes a raw
# frame log to, one file per task (ds-<task>.log), and the batch client's raw
# responses (batch-<time>.log; no audio, no key). Read with os.Getenv in
# internal/transcribe, not through internal/config; .env still sets it, because
# the loader exports every line it reads. Empty writes nothing.
#AICC_TRANSCRIBE_WIRE=

# On qwen this is effectively required: the workspace id is part of the
# hostname, so there is no default that could work.
#   qwen:   wss://<workspace-id>.cn-beijing.maas.aliyuncs.com/api-ws/v1/inference
#   openai: wss://api.openai.com/v1/realtime?intent=transcription
#AICC_TRANSCRIBE_ENDPOINT=

# Overrides the client's default model (qwen: qwen-audio-3.1-asr-flash-message).
# The value must match the client. On qwen the accepted families are
# qwen-audio-<ver>-asr-flash-streaming, qwen-audio-<ver>-asr-flash-message,
# fun-asr-realtime* and fun-asr-flash-8k-realtime*; any other name, including
# a typo, is refused at startup. qwen3-asr-flash-realtime speaks the OpenAI
# dialect and will not run on the qwen client.
#AICC_TRANSCRIBE_MODEL=

#
# The text model (issue #138; #90 reuses it)
#
# One OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions endpoint for work that is plain text in,
# text out, configured apart from the voice provider above. Off unless the
# endpoint and model are both set and TEXT_LLM_API_KEY holds a key (a key is
# read from the environment by name, like OPENAI_API_KEY; it is not an AICC_*
# setting). The endpoint is the API base without /chat/completions, for example
#   https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1
#AICC_TEXT_LLM_ENDPOINT=
#AICC_TEXT_LLM_MODEL=
#TEXT_LLM_API_KEY=

# One job that uses the model gives up after this long.
#AICC_TEXT_LLM_TIMEOUT=20s

# The most characters one prompt may total, instructions included. A transcript
# longer than this is shortened (an AI phase first, then the oldest of the
# agent's lines); a call whose prompt still cannot fit gets no model output.
#AICC_TEXT_LLM_CONTEXT_CHARS=24000

# AI draft of after-call work. When an agent's wrap-up begins, the model reads
# that agent's part of the live transcript and drafts the disposition and note
# for the agent to confirm or edit. Needs the text model above and
# AICC_TRANSCRIPTION_ENABLED=true (the agent's part of a call is only
# transcribed by the live tap). Where either is missing the wrap-up form opens
# as it always did.
#AICC_WRAPUP_DRAFT_ENABLED=true

#
# Post-call transcription of recordings (design 08 section 18)
#
# Transcribes a finished call's stereo recording with a batch ASR model, one
# request per channel per clip of at most 4.5 minutes, and stores the result
# beside (never inside) the live transcript. Off by default. It runs only for
# calls whose live transcript does not already cover both channels, and it is
# billed by audio minute. The key is ALIYUN_API_KEY.
#AICC_POSTCALL_TRANSCRIBE_ENABLED=false

# Required when enabled. The HTTPS generation endpoint on the workspace host
# (not the wss:// one above):
#   https://<workspace-id>.cn-beijing.maas.aliyuncs.com/api/v1/services/aigc/multimodal-generation/generation
#AICC_POSTCALL_TRANSCRIBE_ENDPOINT=

#AICC_POSTCALL_TRANSCRIBE_MODEL=qwen-audio-3.1-asr-flash

# Recordings outside these bounds (seconds) are not transcribed automatically.
# A manual request ignores the minimum but not the maximum; 0 is no maximum.
#AICC_POSTCALL_TRANSCRIBE_MIN_SEC=10
#AICC_POSTCALL_TRANSCRIBE_MAX_SEC=3600

# The most audio sent to the vendor in any rolling 24 hours, in minutes. A
# recording that would pass it waits (or, on a manual request, is refused with
# RATE_LIMITED). 0 is no cap. Soft by up to CONCURRENCY recordings.
#AICC_POSTCALL_TRANSCRIBE_DAILY_MAX_MIN=600

# Recordings in flight at once, and attempts per run (backoff 30 s, then 2 min,
# then 8 min) before it is FAILED.
#AICC_POSTCALL_TRANSCRIBE_CONCURRENCY=2
#AICC_POSTCALL_TRANSCRIBE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

#
# Outbound calling
#
# Required to place AI outbound calls: how this deployment reaches a carrier,
# with %s standing for the number. There is no default — the one that used to
# be here routed through the dialplan with loopback, which looks like it works
# and is not: origination_uuid pins the loopback's own A leg, the customer is
# reached on the B leg bridged out beyond it, and the A leg is gone by the time
# the bot has anything to say about the caller. Every stamp and the transfer
# itself then fail with "No such channel!", so an AI outbound call can never be
# handed to a person (C47). Name the gateway instead:
#   sofia/gateway/<gw>/%s
# Unset, placing an AI outbound call is refused and says why. A set value must
# be a sofia/ dial string holding exactly one %s and no other % verb; anything
# else (loopback included) refuses startup.
#AICC_OUTBOUND_ENDPOINT=
# How long an outbound leg (AI outbound and click-to-dial) rings before the
# switch gives up, sent as originate_timeout. The originator holds each leg's
# continuation for a 60s answer window, so this must be at least 5s and
# strictly below 60s or startup is refused. An answer that still arrives after
# the window is hung up (cause ALLOTTED_TIMEOUT) rather than left parked.
# Go duration.
#AICC_OUTBOUND_RING_TIMEOUT=45s
# SIP headers added to the AI outbound customer leg only (not click-to-dial,
# not the bridge to the bot): Name=value pairs separated by ';'. Names must
# start with X- and may not be in the X-AICC- namespace; names are unique
# (case-insensitive); values are non-empty, with no control characters, braces
# or ';'. A value cannot contain ';' because it is the separator, and an empty
# segment or a pair without '=' refuses startup. Unset adds nothing. Behind
# FreeSBC, whose dialplan stamps this marker on click-to-dial legs, the AI leg
# needs the same one:
#   AICC_OUTBOUND_SIP_HEADERS=X-FSBC-Out=1
#AICC_OUTBOUND_SIP_HEADERS=
# Caller ID presented on click-to-dial customer legs. Empty leaves it to the
# switch.
#AICC_OUTBOUND_CLID=
# Country calling code (1 to 3 digits, no +) that completes a national number
# before it is compared with the do-not-call list and before a contact is
# imported, so a number dialled as 2125550101 matches a list entry saved as
# +12125550101. Default 1 (the North American numbering plan). A deployment
# outside +1 sets its own, e.g. 86, and its national numbers then complete to
# +86...; otherwise Chinese numbers are written with +86 or 0086. A number
# written with + or 00 is always taken as written. It also decides which numbers
# the bot's take_message callback numbers treat as domestic. The value cannot be
# blanked: empty means unset, and unset means the default.
#AICC_DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE=1
# How the campaign dialer writes a number to the trunk: E164 (default, dials
# +<cc><number>), E164_NO_PLUS (<cc><number>) or NATIONAL (the number without
# +<cc>, where cc is the campaign's defaultCountryCode, else
# AICC_DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE; a number from another country is dialled as E164
# and logged once per campaign). Campaigns store and compare E.164 whatever
# this is; the CDR's "to" is the dialled string. A single createCall is dialled
# as sent. Pick what your trunk accepts: the development trunk (FreeSBC behind
# sofia/gateway/pstn_gateway) answers 480 to +8618688886666 and 8618688886666
# and connects 18688886666, so it needs NATIONAL. A trunk that expects E.164
# keeps the default. NATIONAL does not restore a trunk prefix such as the 0 of
# a landline area code (known limit). Any other value refuses startup. The bot's
# take_message writes a callback number it was given in this form too, with
# AICC_DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE deciding which numbers are domestic; under NATIONAL
# a domestic landline keeps its 0 there.
#AICC_OUTBOUND_DIAL_FORM=E164
# Outer calling hours for campaigns: the callee's local time of day outside
# which no campaign dials, whatever its own calling window says. A campaign's
# window must lie wholly inside them (refused on create and on every change)
# and the dialer checks again before each call. HH:MM-HH:MM, every day; the
# range may cross midnight (20:00-04:00). Applies to campaigns only: a single
# call does not know the callee's time zone.
#AICC_OUTBOUND_HOURS=08:00-21:00
# Campaign breakers (design 10 section 7.4). Consecutive attempts in one campaign
# ending TRANSIENT or ORIGINATE_FAILED pause it as TRUNK_FAILURE; consecutive
# answered calls ending BOT_FAILED pause it as PROVIDER_FAILURE. 1 to 1000.
#AICC_TRUNK_BREAKER_COUNT=20
#AICC_PROVIDER_BREAKER_COUNT=5
# The dialer (design 10 section 5.2). At most this many AI outbound legs ring at
# once across all campaigns, whatever each campaign's maxConcurrent says. 1 to
# 100000. A campaign is also held back while bot sessions in use, reserved or
# waiting, plus the answers its ringing legs are expected to bring (the answer
# rate is estimated per campaign, and taken as 1 until 50 rung attempts), would
# leave fewer than the inbound reserve free (see AICC_BOT_INBOUND_RESERVE), and
# while its DID is at its maxOutboundChannels.
#AICC_OUTBOUND_MAX_RINGING=100
# After any campaign reached a number (the call was answered, rang out, was
# busy or rejected) no other campaign dials it for this long; its contacts wait
# in the other campaigns. Go duration, 0 (off) to 720h.
#AICC_CAMPAIGN_FREQUENCY_WINDOW=24h

#
# Recordings
#
# FS keeps files where the switch wrote them and serves them from
# AICC_RECORDING_DIR. S3 serves from any S3-compatible store; the switch's
# files reach it either through the AICC_RECORDING_DIR spool (ingestion
# uploads and clears it) or directly, when the switch records through
# mod_http_cache straight into the store (deploy/dev runs SeaweedFS for this:
# filer on 127.0.0.1:8888 for the switch, S3 on 127.0.0.1:8333 for the app)
# — then no spool is needed and ingestion only confirms the object arrived.
#AICC_RECORDING_DIR=
# FS or S3.
#AICC_RECORDING_BACKEND=FS
#AICC_S3_ENDPOINT=
#AICC_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
#AICC_S3_SECRET_KEY=
#AICC_S3_BUCKET=aicc-recordings
#AICC_S3_SSL=false

#
# Sessions
#
# Must be >= 1m or the server refuses to start.
#AICC_SESSION_TTL=12h
#AICC_SESSION_COOKIE=aicc_session
# Set true wherever the SPA is served over HTTPS. Dev is HTTP-only, so the
# default is false and the cookie would otherwise never be sent.
#AICC_SECURE_COOKIES=false

#
# Machine access
#
# There is no API-key setting any more. A key used to be one shared secret in
# this file, reaching exactly two operations and standing in for a supervisor
# nobody could name. Keys are managed credentials now: issued through
# POST /api-keys, each with its own name and its own scopes, revocable one at a
# time and recorded in the audit trail as itself. Nothing to configure here.

#
# CDR webhooks
#
# Finished calls are delivered to whatever webhook subscriptions exist
# (System -> Webhooks, created through the API). These two bound the delivery
# record, and unlike recording retention they are ON by default: a recording is
# irreplaceable customer audio, while a delivery row is operational exhaust that
# grows with every call forever.
#
# Two windows because the rows are read at different ages — a delivered one
# within hours of a customer asking "did you send it", a failed one weeks later
# when somebody reconciles a month and finds a gap. 0 keeps that kind for good.
# PENDING is never swept: a delivery still owed is work, not history.
#AICC_WEBHOOK_RETENTION_DELIVERED_DAYS=7
#AICC_WEBHOOK_RETENTION_FAILED_DAYS=30

#
# Observability
#
# debug, info, warn or error.
#AICC_LOG_LEVEL=info
# One log file per process start, named by start time, alongside stderr.
# Cannot be turned off by assigning an empty value — see the note at the top.
#AICC_LOG_DIR=logs
# OTLP/HTTP trace exporter. Empty installs no exporter and tracing costs
# almost nothing.
#AICC_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
#AICC_SERVICE_NAME=aicc

#
# Extensions
#
# The pool an agent's or supervisor's phone number is allocated from when an
# account is created, written low-high and inclusive at both ends. One pool for
# both roles. Allocation takes the lowest free number in the range, so a number
# freed by a departing agent is handed out again rather than the pool drifting
# upwards for ever; a range that does not parse refuses to start rather than
# quietly falling back to this default.
#AICC_EXTENSION_RANGE=1000-1999
# The pool a queue's number is allocated from when a queue is created without
# one. Separate from the extension pool because the leading digit is how an
# operator reads what they are calling. A queue is dialled, never registered
# as, so its number lives on the queue and not in the extensions table.
#AICC_QUEUE_RANGE=7000-7999

# How many days a recording is kept before the retention sweep deletes both the
# stored object and the row's audio. 0 keeps everything for ever, which is the
# default — a deployment that upgrades into this must not start deleting audio
# because nobody set a number. The sweep runs hourly, deletes the object first
# and only then marks the row, so a failure leaves a recording that cannot be
# played rather than bytes nobody is paying attention to.
#AICC_RECORDING_RETENTION_DAYS=0

#
# Seed data
#
# demo inserts demo accounts, queues, six published bilingual flows (95001/95002
# novanet_support, 95011/95012 mobile_support, and 95021 to 95052 for the
# other four), and seven days of history into an empty ledger; existing data
# always wins, so a second boot changes nothing.
# fresh removes that same demo dataset and nothing else — the way back to an
# empty product once a database exists. Calls placed against the demo stay.
# Empty, demo or fresh; anything else refuses to start.
#AICC_SEED=
# The password demo gives the accounts and the static extensions it creates;
# accounts and extensions that already exist keep theirs. At least 8
# characters when AICC_SEED=demo. The stack also makes it the switch's
# default_password, written into XML, so & < > " ' whitespace and control
# characters refuse to start.
#AICC_SEED_PASSWORD=aicc@123

#
# Test-only. The server never reads these; they gate tests that cost money or
# need infrastructure.
#
# Runs the live provider tests, which spend real API credit and need the keys
# above: AICC_LIVE_PROVIDER_TEST=1 go test ./internal/provider/ -run Live -v
#AICC_LIVE_PROVIDER_TEST=
# Runs the S3 storage round-trip against a real S3-compatible store.
#AICC_S3_TEST=
